  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given $f(x)=4x-6$ and $g(x)=5x-10,$ find  $(fg)(4).$",
  "worked_solution": "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(4),$ we find $f(4)$ and $g(4),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(4)$ and $g(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =4(4)-6=10 \\\\ g(4) & =5(4)-10=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(4)=f(4)g(4)=10\\cdot 10=100\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(4x-6)(5x-10) \\\\  & =20{x}^{2}-70x+60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(4) & =20(4)^{2}-70(4)+60 \\\\  & =320-280+60 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$",
